---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';
const path = '/use-cases/browse-abandonment';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'use-cases/browse-abandonment');
const desc = description(site, 'use-cases/browse-abandonment');
const comparisonApps = [
  'sequenzy', 'klaviyo', 'drip', 'omnisend', 'yotpo-email-sms', 'shopify-email',
  'mailchimp', 'activecampaign', 'brevo', 'firepush',
].map((s) => appBySlug(s)!).filter(Boolean);
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<article class="container prose-shopify py-16">
<a href="/use-cases">All use cases</a>
<h1>Browse follow-up without cart-style pressure</h1>
<p>A product view is a research signal, not a checkout or permission to text. Build a follow-up only where you can identify an eligible subscriber, observe a reliable event, and explain why the content helps. The examples below are proposed exercises, not measured merchant outcomes.</p>
<h2>Prove the event before writing the sequence</h2><p>Record the product and variant identifiers, event time, identity source, and market. Check whether the event represents a page load, repeat view, or collection visit. Anonymous views should not become guessed phone matches. Reproduce the event on a test profile and inspect what actually arrives in the sending tool; a Shopify integration label does not prove browser tracking is configured.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.klaviyo.com/hc/en-us/articles/115002775252">Klaviyo's browse-flow documentation</a> describes a Viewed Product flow. Treat that as a specific implementation reference, not evidence that every listed app receives equivalent events or supports the same filters.</p>
<h2>Make higher-intent activity end the reminder</h2><p>Before dispatch, check for a later cart, checkout, purchase, unsubscribe, or another browse message. Choose a clear priority rule so welcome and browse do not both issue an introductory offer. Deduplicate repeated views and decide whether a new view replaces the pending product or is ignored during a cooldown. Store the decision and event identifier so retries do not create extra messages.</p>
<h2>Choose product or collection context deliberately</h2><p>A researcher comparing ring settings may need sizing, certification details, or an appointment link. A pet-food shopper may need the ingredient panel and life-stage guidance. Someone viewing several living-room items may benefit from a collection edit rather than a reminder for the last incidental click. Do not claim a product is personally suitable based only on browsing.</p>
<h2>Set content and timing as hypotheses</h2><p>Start with a useful product recap, one supported proof point, and a link back to the relevant page. Test whether a delay fits the purchase cycle rather than presenting a universal number of views or hours as proven. Financing, warranty, dimensions, and compatibility can answer objections without a code. A discount needs a separate margin decision; repeated visits alone do not establish price sensitivity.</p>
<h2>Handle stale catalog data</h2><p>Revalidate variant availability, price, and URL at send time where possible. If the selected product disappears, skip or use a reviewed category fallback without implying it is the item viewed. Keep gift and sensitive-category copy discreet: a shared phone or inbox may expose the message to someone else.</p>
<h2>Evaluate the program</h2><p>Proposed acceptance cases include repeat views in one session, a purchase during the wait, a removed variant, two devices resolving to one profile, and consent withdrawal. Log eligible entries, suppression reasons, duplicates, sends, opt-outs, and later orders. Use an assigned holdout if volume supports it; attributed purchases alone do not establish incremental value.</p>
<p><a href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Cart recovery</a> owns checkout and recovery-link decisions. <a href="/use-cases/analytics">Analytics</a> owns experiment denominators and contribution accounting. Browse follow-up owns research relevance and the handoff to stronger intent.</p>
<h2>App discovery and demonstration checklist</h2>
<p>Use these profiles as starting points. Ask each supplier to demonstrate this workflow with your events, markets, permissions, and failure cases before buying. Listing is not a verified feature comparison or performance ranking; confirm current plans and destination charges directly.</p>
<ul>{comparisonApps.map(app => <li><a href={`/apps/${app.slug}`}>{app.name}</a></li>)}</ul>
</article>
</BaseLayout>
